1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,680
that you can change your own incentives.

2
00:00:02,680 --> 00:00:05,560
So you realize, look, if the two of us each do our own thing,

3
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:07,400
it's going to be bad for both of us.

4
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:08,820
But we can sign a contract where we both

5
00:00:08,820 --> 00:00:11,200
agree to take each other's interests into account.

6
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:12,840
And then this will be good for us.

7
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:14,280
And someone says, well, yeah, but why

8
00:00:14,280 --> 00:00:15,940
is putting your name on a piece of paper

9
00:00:15,940 --> 00:00:17,440
make you do what's good for me?

10
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:19,960
Well, part of signing the contract

11
00:00:19,960 --> 00:00:22,960
is that once you sign it, you are making yourself

12
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:26,120
legally vulnerable to penalties or punishment

13
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:28,560
if you don't do what you promise.

14
00:00:28,560 --> 00:00:31,800
So you are essentially changing the game.

15
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:33,600
You're changing the payoffs for your game.

16
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:36,980
Thank you.

17
00:00:36,980 --> 00:00:37,480
There you are.

18
00:00:43,040 --> 00:00:45,520
And what's interesting is when you really think about it,

19
00:00:45,520 --> 00:00:47,800
with enforceable contracts can be way better

20
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:49,400
than trigger strategies.

21
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:51,600
Because trigger strategies, basically all you can do

22
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:55,600
is just go back to the pre-agreement situation.

23
00:00:55,600 --> 00:00:59,320
But with enforceable contracts, you would go and say,

24
00:00:59,320 --> 00:01:01,160
you will go and be punished.

25
00:01:01,160 --> 00:01:02,520
You owe me $1 million.

26
00:01:02,520 --> 00:01:03,600
You owe me all the harm.

27
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:05,840
You owe me more than the harm.

28
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:08,720
So that's possible.

29
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:12,120
So after the midterm, we'll talk a bit about some strange limits

30
00:01:12,120 --> 00:01:13,160
on enforceable contracts.

31
00:01:13,160 --> 00:01:14,360
This is worth pointing out.

32
00:01:14,360 --> 00:01:16,960
So in the US legal system, and in general

33
00:01:16,960 --> 00:01:18,960
in the Anglo-American tradition, I

34
00:01:18,960 --> 00:01:21,200
guess there was a controversy about using this phrase,

35
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:23,160
but it's a technical phrase in the law,

36
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:26,880
referring to laws descending from England, which

37
00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:30,280
some countries with almost no English people still use.

38
00:01:30,280 --> 00:01:33,840
And other countries with a lot of English people don't use.

39
00:01:33,840 --> 00:01:36,120
Anyway.

40
00:01:36,120 --> 00:01:38,480
So in this tradition, there is a strange rule

41
00:01:38,480 --> 00:01:40,280
which has been around for a long time saying

42
00:01:40,280 --> 00:01:42,680
you are not allowed to put a penalty clause in a contract.

43
00:01:46,080 --> 00:01:47,800
Now, you say, what does that mean,

44
00:01:47,800 --> 00:01:50,160
that you can never put something into a contract that

45
00:01:50,160 --> 00:01:51,680
penalizes someone?

46
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:54,280
No, because there are lawyers involved,

47
00:01:54,280 --> 00:01:56,960
and there's a fine difference between a penalty clause

48
00:01:56,960 --> 00:01:58,960
and something that penalizes someone.

49
00:01:58,960 --> 00:02:01,320
They're totally different things.

50
00:02:01,320 --> 00:02:03,240
Like, well, how would you know the difference?

51
00:02:03,240 --> 00:02:04,440
Well, here's one way.

52
00:02:04,440 --> 00:02:06,440
If there's a section of the contract that says,

53
00:02:06,440 --> 00:02:10,560
the penalty clause shall hereby be X, then you're out of luck.

54
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:12,360
You have used the wrong language,

55
00:02:12,360 --> 00:02:16,680
and then that will not be enforced by the courts.

56
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:18,220
So that's one thing to not do if you

57
00:02:18,220 --> 00:02:19,680
want to put in a penalty clause.

58
00:02:19,680 --> 00:02:21,360
But what else?

59
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:23,920
Well, it just seems too much like if there's

60
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:27,240
something where it says you owe me twice the harm.

61
00:02:27,240 --> 00:02:28,360
Say, wait a second.

62
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:29,720
Twice the harm?

63
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:31,880
That seems like one more times the harm

64
00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:36,160
than you would get if you weren't penalizing the person.

65
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:37,800
So in general, anything that says

66
00:02:37,800 --> 00:02:40,680
you can go and you owe them all the harm they've done,

67
00:02:40,680 --> 00:02:41,920
then that's fine.

68
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:44,040
Because then you're not, if there's not a penalty,

69
00:02:44,040 --> 00:02:46,120
then you're just making it up to them.

70
00:02:46,120 --> 00:02:49,240
But if it says you have to pay them more than you've harmed

71
00:02:49,240 --> 00:02:51,240
them, that's a problem.

72
00:02:51,240 --> 00:02:55,120
Now, again, if you have a smart lawyer, they will just say,

73
00:02:55,120 --> 00:02:59,680
well, we'll just stipulate that we say the harm is $3 million.

74
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:02,080
And if the business says, yeah, but the harm is actually

75
00:03:02,080 --> 00:03:04,480
only $1 billion.

76
00:03:04,480 --> 00:03:06,480
Sorry, I couldn't hear what you just said there.

77
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:07,520
That was garbled.

78
00:03:07,520 --> 00:03:10,040
Because if that was what you were saying,

79
00:03:10,040 --> 00:03:12,560
then there would be a legal problem

80
00:03:12,560 --> 00:03:14,040
if we were enforcing this.

81
00:03:14,040 --> 00:03:16,520
And there indeed is one.

82
00:03:16,520 --> 00:03:18,640
So you didn't say that.

83
00:03:18,640 --> 00:03:21,280
That kind of mentality.

84
00:03:21,280 --> 00:03:23,880
All right, now, Robin Hanson, in his dissertation,

85
00:03:23,880 --> 00:03:26,680
had a chapter on how strange it is that you can't

86
00:03:26,680 --> 00:03:29,440
have an explicit penalty clause.

87
00:03:29,440 --> 00:03:31,320
And again, with the whole point of a penalty,

88
00:03:31,320 --> 00:03:33,600
why would you want to have a penalty clause?

89
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:36,840
Because you want to give someone a really strong incentive

90
00:03:36,840 --> 00:03:38,760
to not betray you.

91
00:03:38,760 --> 00:03:41,960
It's very important to you that they cooperate.

92
00:03:41,960 --> 00:03:43,960
And again, why would it be so important?

93
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:45,320
Well, here's an obvious reason.

94
00:03:45,320 --> 00:03:48,200
What if a lot of times they can fail to cooperate

95
00:03:48,200 --> 00:03:50,640
and you won't notice?

96
00:03:50,640 --> 00:03:53,720
Suppose there's only a 10% chance of you noticing.

97
00:03:53,720 --> 00:03:55,760
So if you catch them, they owe you the damages.

98
00:03:55,760 --> 00:03:57,520
And if you don't catch them, what do they

99
00:03:57,520 --> 00:03:59,920
have to pay you by definition?

100
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:01,200
Nothing.

101
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:03,360
So that is a case where it would make a lot of sense

102
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:04,720
to have a penalty clause.

103
00:04:04,720 --> 00:04:07,320
Totally understandable, great business reasoning,

104
00:04:07,320 --> 00:04:08,800
great efficiency reasoning.

105
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:10,760
And yet the Anglo-American legal tradition

106
00:04:10,760 --> 00:04:13,960
says, no, that's not allowed.

107
00:04:13,960 --> 00:04:14,460
Yes?

108
00:04:14,460 --> 00:04:18,480
What about a warranty, so there are warranties where if a

109
00:04:18,480 --> 00:04:21,040
product is malfunctioning, you owe someone?

110
00:04:21,040 --> 00:04:21,920
Yeah, yes.

111
00:04:21,920 --> 00:04:24,440
So here's the other thing about the system, which

112
00:04:24,440 --> 00:04:27,440
is that if a firm offered you that

113
00:04:27,440 --> 00:04:33,160
and you took them to court, then by the letter of the law,

114
00:04:33,160 --> 00:04:35,320
you could probably only get the original money back.

115
00:04:35,320 --> 00:04:37,320
In practice, since there are almost no warranties

116
00:04:37,320 --> 00:04:38,440
that would be enforced by court, it's

117
00:04:38,440 --> 00:04:39,800
just a matter of reputation.

118
00:04:39,800 --> 00:04:41,400
You go to them, say you set double the money back,

119
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:42,600
and they give it to you.

120
00:04:42,600 --> 00:04:45,160
So again, it's legal to offer it.

121
00:04:45,160 --> 00:04:47,120
So it's not that you go to jail for going and putting

122
00:04:47,120 --> 00:04:48,720
a penalty clause in the contract.

123
00:04:48,720 --> 00:04:50,560
It's just the court will not acknowledge it.

124
00:04:54,160 --> 00:04:57,400
So anyway, after the midterm, we'll talk about this.

125
00:04:57,400 --> 00:05:00,480
But it does seem like this is a strange limitation

126
00:05:00,480 --> 00:05:03,000
on people's ability to go and voluntarily solve

127
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:04,640
their cooperation problems by contract.

128
00:05:07,240 --> 00:05:09,520
So questions about enforceable contracts

129
00:05:09,520 --> 00:05:12,480
and how really the principle, like enforceable contracts

130
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:16,640
would allow you to turn any game into any other game.

131
00:05:16,640 --> 00:05:18,480
So you're bound to be in a prisoner's limits.

132
00:05:18,480 --> 00:05:23,080
So you both sign a contract saying that if you go and don't

133
00:05:23,080 --> 00:05:24,880
cooperate, then you owe me $1 million.

134
00:05:24,880 --> 00:05:28,240
And then you go and redraw the entire payoff matrix.

135
00:05:28,240 --> 00:05:30,640
Really, a contract could just be to say,

136
00:05:30,640 --> 00:05:32,280
here's the original payoff matrix.

137
00:05:32,280 --> 00:05:33,900
Here's the revised payoff matrix based

138
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:35,520
upon the terms of this contract.

139
00:05:35,520 --> 00:05:38,040
And then you could make cooperation strictly dominant.

140
00:05:38,040 --> 00:05:42,920
And all problems could be solved that way, the principle.

141
00:05:42,920 --> 00:05:44,000
All right.

142
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,000
Now, when you look at experiments, funny thing

143
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:49,480
is that we generally see way too much cooperation.

144
00:05:49,480 --> 00:05:52,640
We see way more cooperation than you get from repeated play,

145
00:05:52,640 --> 00:05:55,640
especially as people cooperate in one-shot games.

146
00:05:55,640 --> 00:05:57,440
And we also see a lot more cooperation

147
00:05:57,440 --> 00:05:59,280
than codes would allow, because people often

148
00:05:59,280 --> 00:06:01,800
cooperate without negotiation.

149
00:06:01,800 --> 00:06:04,120
So odd.

150
00:06:04,120 --> 00:06:05,080
What should we learn?

151
00:06:05,080 --> 00:06:06,200
What should we do?

152
00:06:06,200 --> 00:06:07,880
What should we think about this?

153
00:06:07,880 --> 00:06:10,160
Well, here's the obvious answer.

154
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:14,120
People aren't as selfish as economists assume.

155
00:06:14,120 --> 00:06:15,660
There's other explanations, but this

156
00:06:15,660 --> 00:06:17,800
seems like a pretty obvious one.

157
00:06:17,800 --> 00:06:21,800
Why would people cooperate when it's not in their interests?

158
00:06:21,800 --> 00:06:23,480
They don't always act in their interest.

159
00:06:23,480 --> 00:06:26,360
Another one would be they don't understand the game,

160
00:06:26,360 --> 00:06:27,520
which is possible.

161
00:06:27,520 --> 00:06:29,360
Although, if the game is really transparent,

162
00:06:29,360 --> 00:06:31,480
like a dictator game, what can you tell them?

163
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:32,360
Here's the game.

164
00:06:32,360 --> 00:06:34,640
You'll give as much money as you want to the other guy.

165
00:06:34,640 --> 00:06:35,200
The end.

166
00:06:35,200 --> 00:06:37,440
How many people will not understand that game?

167
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:39,120
Seems like a super obvious game.

168
00:06:39,120 --> 00:06:41,240
In fact, we all play that game all day.

169
00:06:41,240 --> 00:06:43,840
Whenever you don't give your money away to other people,

170
00:06:43,840 --> 00:06:44,880
you're playing this game.

171
00:06:48,680 --> 00:06:50,520
So questions?

172
00:06:50,520 --> 00:06:53,040
Now, something kind of interesting about this is,

173
00:06:53,040 --> 00:06:54,440
if you think that what's going on

174
00:06:54,440 --> 00:06:57,000
is that people are cooperating more than they normally would

175
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:59,040
because they're not selfish, then we

176
00:06:59,040 --> 00:07:01,560
might actually get a more optimistic result

177
00:07:01,560 --> 00:07:03,120
than I was suggesting earlier.

178
00:07:03,120 --> 00:07:07,360
Because remember before I was saying that basically anything

179
00:07:07,360 --> 00:07:12,000
that leads to reduced collusion in an industry

180
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:13,560
will also lead to reduced cooperation

181
00:07:13,560 --> 00:07:15,320
in public goods games.

182
00:07:15,320 --> 00:07:17,080
Because they're basically the same model.

183
00:07:17,080 --> 00:07:20,160
It's not that model is just the same as Cornell.

184
00:07:20,160 --> 00:07:22,200
So you might say, look, any story

185
00:07:22,200 --> 00:07:26,160
about how it's possible to get a good outcome in Cornell

186
00:07:26,160 --> 00:07:31,240
means that you're likely to get a good outcome in Cornell.

187
00:07:31,240 --> 00:07:33,120
Also implies that voluntary public goods

188
00:07:33,120 --> 00:07:35,040
permission is not going to work.

189
00:07:35,040 --> 00:07:36,500
And on the other hand, if you say

190
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:39,360
that you are able to go and that collusion,

191
00:07:39,360 --> 00:07:42,440
that you are able to go and get voluntary solutions

192
00:07:42,440 --> 00:07:45,880
to public goods games, it also seems equally plausible

193
00:07:45,880 --> 00:07:48,720
that you would get voluntary solutions to cartels

194
00:07:48,720 --> 00:07:51,400
and that cartels would work.

195
00:07:51,400 --> 00:07:53,320
So this is an argument that I had first

196
00:07:53,320 --> 00:07:56,360
had with Tyler 25 years ago.

197
00:07:56,360 --> 00:07:58,280
Anyway, so I do have a paper with that string

198
00:07:58,280 --> 00:07:59,840
in where we show why he's wrong.

199
00:07:59,840 --> 00:08:03,200
And here, in the real world, which

200
00:08:03,200 --> 00:08:05,320
is the really important place to be wrong or right,

201
00:08:05,320 --> 00:08:06,560
is the real world.

202
00:08:06,560 --> 00:08:08,240
And this says, look, if the reason why

203
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:12,000
we get extra cooperation is that people aren't selfish,

204
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:15,560
then we actually need to look at what people consider

205
00:08:15,560 --> 00:08:18,400
to be selfish or unselfish in a given situation.

206
00:08:18,400 --> 00:08:20,440
So when you are raising money for charity

207
00:08:20,440 --> 00:08:23,200
to go and help people, what is generally

208
00:08:23,200 --> 00:08:26,360
considered to be the unselfish thing to do?

209
00:08:26,360 --> 00:08:27,160
Donate.

210
00:08:27,160 --> 00:08:28,660
So in that case, we'd expect that we

211
00:08:28,660 --> 00:08:32,360
will get more donations than you'd expect given selfishness.

212
00:08:32,360 --> 00:08:34,280
On the other hand, what is generally

213
00:08:34,280 --> 00:08:36,280
considered the selfish thing to do

214
00:08:36,280 --> 00:08:41,640
when you are trying to get a collusion on an AIDS drug, say?

215
00:08:41,640 --> 00:08:44,360
What is considered the unselfish thing to do?

216
00:08:44,360 --> 00:08:45,560
The unselfish thing?

217
00:08:45,560 --> 00:08:46,400
The unselfish thing.

218
00:08:46,400 --> 00:08:47,400
The unselfish thing.

219
00:08:47,400 --> 00:08:51,160
Like the compassionate, kind, decent thing to do.

220
00:08:51,160 --> 00:08:52,600
Not collude.

221
00:08:52,600 --> 00:08:53,100
Right.

222
00:08:53,100 --> 00:08:56,960
So if you think that the extra cooperation is being caused

223
00:08:56,960 --> 00:09:00,400
by unselfish motivation, this is a reason

224
00:09:00,400 --> 00:09:02,440
to actually think that things that people actually

225
00:09:02,440 --> 00:09:04,640
view favorably, like donating to charity,

226
00:09:04,640 --> 00:09:07,120
actually you will get good outcomes.

227
00:09:07,120 --> 00:09:09,000
Things that people socially view unfavorably,

228
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,720
like trying to restrict the supply of food

229
00:09:11,720 --> 00:09:14,520
in order to get more money for you and starve children,

230
00:09:14,520 --> 00:09:16,720
that will actually work no better than expected.

231
00:09:16,720 --> 00:09:18,480
Or maybe it would be worse than expected.

232
00:09:18,480 --> 00:09:21,280
People's consciences might bother them.

233
00:09:21,280 --> 00:09:23,200
And then once you start thinking about this,

234
00:09:23,200 --> 00:09:24,600
you want to think, well, are there

235
00:09:24,600 --> 00:09:27,000
any cartels that are moralized?

236
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:29,020
Moralized cartels where the members actually

237
00:09:29,020 --> 00:09:32,160
regard someone who goes and produces more and cuts prices

238
00:09:32,160 --> 00:09:33,000
being evil.

239
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:33,640
Unions?

240
00:09:33,640 --> 00:09:34,140
Yeah.

241
00:09:34,140 --> 00:09:35,880
So unions are a good example.

242
00:09:35,880 --> 00:09:38,160
So I remember once I was in New York on May Day,

243
00:09:38,160 --> 00:09:39,580
and there was a big May Day parade

244
00:09:39,580 --> 00:09:40,960
that I happened to wander across.

245
00:09:40,960 --> 00:09:44,960
And they had a 15-foot inflatable rat.

246
00:09:44,960 --> 00:09:47,240
And there was a big sign on the rat that said,

247
00:09:47,240 --> 00:09:50,000
don't be a rat scab.

248
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:52,080
So this was a case where they had actually

249
00:09:52,080 --> 00:09:54,480
embraced the norm of, it's not just

250
00:09:54,480 --> 00:09:56,360
that it makes us more money.

251
00:09:56,360 --> 00:09:58,960
It's the morally good thing to do.

252
00:09:58,960 --> 00:10:01,880
Whereas when Swiss executives fixed the price of aspirin

253
00:10:01,880 --> 00:10:04,160
and ended up in jail in Texas, there's

254
00:10:04,160 --> 00:10:05,880
no report that when they were doing this,

255
00:10:05,880 --> 00:10:07,880
they were saying, we must do this

256
00:10:07,880 --> 00:10:10,640
for the good of aspirin workers all over the world.

257
00:10:10,640 --> 00:10:14,440
They're all counting on us and our selfless sacrifice.

258
00:10:14,440 --> 00:10:15,120
That was not it.

259
00:10:15,120 --> 00:10:17,680
It was like, all right, so we'll go and make more money

260
00:10:17,680 --> 00:10:18,840
with our aspirin.

261
00:10:18,840 --> 00:10:19,840
Oh, no.

262
00:10:19,840 --> 00:10:21,320
That's not it.

263
00:10:21,320 --> 00:10:23,920
They did not seem to see themselves as heroes.

264
00:10:23,920 --> 00:10:26,520
Whereas, yes, so in unions, they may see themselves as a hero.

265
00:10:26,520 --> 00:10:28,600
Sometimes farmers actually seem to see themselves

266
00:10:28,600 --> 00:10:30,120
as a relic when they cut production

